    description: "Historic abbeys need structural assessment, seismic retrofit, and discreet modern systems. Learn how engineers preserve these sacred buildings for centuries.",
    h1: "What Engineering Keeps a Historic Abbey Standing and Safe?",
    answer: "A historic abbey has already survived centuries — the engineer's job is to make sure it survives centuries more without losing what makes it sacred. The work starts with understanding: archival research, measured survey, material testing, and structural analysis of stone, timber, and early iron that was built by craft rules, not calculations. From there, our licensed engineers design interventions that are minimal, compatible, and reversible where possible — tying walls to floors and roofs, strengthening with lime-compatible mortars and discreet steel or fiber reinforcement, improving drainage that has been undermining foundations for decades. Then come the modern systems the building never had: fire detection and suppression that won't destroy what they're protecting, accessibility that doesn't scar the architecture, and sometimes careful climate control for preservation. I've found abbey work humbling — you're a guest in a building that has outlasted every engineer who ever touched it, and your interventions should be worthy of that.",
