    answer: "A parsonage is a home with a twist: the church owns it, a pastor's family lives in it, and it needs to serve faithfully through many tenures. The engineering is residential at its core — a well-built house with sound structure, efficient systems, and durable finishes — but the ownership model changes the priorities. Durability beats fashion, because the church maintains it for decades. Simplicity beats complexity, because volunteer trustees and rotating leadership need systems anyone can understand. And flexibility matters: the house must suit a single pastor, a family with kids, or serve as a guest house between appointments. Structurally it's conventional residential engineering — foundations, framing, and lateral design per the local code — executed with unusual care for moisture management and longevity. I've seen parsonages that were a blessing to every pastor who lived there and ones that drained the church budget; the difference was build quality and systems chosen for the long haul.",
